  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCleaningLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCleaning"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCorruption",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#InaccuratePatientRecords;OperationalDisruptions"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LowDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SoftwareBugs;HardwareFailures"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data corruption refers to instances where data is altered by mistake or becomes unreadable due to software bugs, hardware failures, or transmission errors. This corruption can lead to inaccurate patient records and operational disruptions, posing significant risks to the delivery of healthcare. Several studies show that even minor corruption can have substantial impacts, as it can compromise the reliability of the entire dataset. Data can become corrupted due to software errors, hardware malfunctions, or during transmission, often resulting in inaccurate or lost patient information and operational disruptions. To mitigate these risks, healthcare systems must employ regular backups, error-checking mechanisms like checksums, and redundant storage solutions to preserve data integrity and ensure continuity of operations.\n\nThe severity of data corruption is often considered high, as it can directly impact clinical decision-making and patient care. For example, corrupted health records could lead to inappropriate medical treatments, as reported by Nemec et al. in their concluding remarks in terms of impacting patient care, digital transformation in healthcare has improved patient care but also increased the vulnerability of sensitive data to cyberattacks. Data corruption can occur due to various reasons, including software errors, hardware failures, and cyberattacks, making it a moderate to high likelihood issue depending on the specific healthcare IT environment. However, detecting data corruption can be challenging, particularly when subtle errors do not immediately manifest as system failures. Mejía-Granda, Carlos M., et al. mentioned as a consequence of security vulnerabilities; The critical vulnerabilities for health with the greatest impact, presence, number of software products affected, and score are majority caused by (i) poor management of credentials enabling unintended actions and lack of control and (ii) buffer out-of-bounds causing data corruption and improper code execution. Hence, robust validation and regular system audits are essential for early detection"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCorruptionPrevention",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/GovernanceAndComplianceJustification"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCorruptionPreventionJustification",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataCorruptionPrevention"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataDuplication",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#IncreasedCosts;ClinicalErrors"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PoorSystemIntegration;ManualEntry"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data corruption refers to instances where data is altered by mistake or becomes unreadable due to software bugs, hardware failures, or transmission errors. This corruption can lead to inaccurate patient records and operational disruptions, posing significant risks to the delivery of healthcare. Several studies show that even minor corruption can have substantial impacts, as it can compromise the reliability of the entire dataset. Data can become corrupted due to software errors, hardware malfunctions, or during transmission, often resulting in inaccurate or lost patient information and operational disruptions. To mitigate these risks, healthcare systems must employ regular backups, error-checking mechanisms like checksums, and redundant storage solutions to preserve data integrity and ensure continuity of operations.\n\n\nWhile often less severe than corruption or loss, data duplication can lead to inefficiencies in storage, increased costs, and complications in data management and retrieval. Conduct regular audits of data storage to identify redundant, obsolete, or trivial (ROT) information. Removing ROT data helps manage storage costs and\nimproves system performance. Given the complex data flows in healthcare systems, especially in interoperable environments, the likelihood of duplication is high. It can be relatively easily detected through data quality management tools and processes that identify and resolve duplicates. Mrudgandha K. described the likelihood and detectability in terms of managing data quality, in a recent report of Acceldata on Data Quality and Reliability, ‘healthcare data coming in from various sources sometimes results in duplicates with discrepancies. To ensure data quality, we need to identify and merge duplicate records while addressing the discrepancies by cross-checking the data across various sources’."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "This category focuses on the initial stage of data handling, where information is first captured or entered into the system. Errors at this stage can have far-reaching consequences, compromising the accuracy, completeness, and reliability of the data."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollectionCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollectionConsequences",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Consequences"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataFragmentation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ImpairedDataAnalysis;Inefficiencies;DelayedCare"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#LackOfIntegration;SystemMergers"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data fragmentation occurs when information is dispersed across various systems without sufficient integration, complicating the retrieval and comprehensive analysis of patient data. Literature highlights the consequences of fragmented data, such as a lack of system integration and incomplete data access, which can lead to incomplete patient records and inefficiencies in data retrieval, potentially delaying patient care. Mitigation strategies involve data integration solutions, the adoption of interoperability standards, and centralized databases to reduce fragmentation.\n\nThe fragmentation of data creates the need to jump between interfaces and manually synthesize information to make decisions, highlighting the severity and operational inefficiencies caused by data fragmentation. It is generally regarded as medium, as fragmentation complicates data management and can hinder the effective delivery of healthcare services by making data less accessible and transparent. Detectability can be high with robust monitoring and auditing tools available, which can help detect integrity issues before they cause significant damage. Despite a heavy emphasis on improving interoperability and implementing standards in recent years, key data elements remain siloed and inaccessible in little pockets across thousands of entities emphasizes the high likelihood of data fragmentation due to the current state of interoperability and data siloing, especially as healthcare systems expand and incorporate more diverse data sources and systems, leading to increased instances of fragmented data storage."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataHazards",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataIntegrityAndValidationFailures",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SignificantClinicalErrors"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LowDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#LackOfReal-timeValidation;OutdatedData"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Maintaining data accuracy and validity is crucial throughout its lifecycle. Systems that do not perform real-time validation or fail to detect duplicates can allow errors to persist. Outdated or inaccurate master data compounds these issues, leading to erroneous entries. Literature on data governance emphasizes the importance of robust validation and regular updates to maintain data integrity, as failures in these areas have been shown to contribute to significant clinical errors.\n\nFailures in data integrity can be extremely severe, as they may lead to incorrect patient treatment by relying on medical decisions based on faulty data. Regarding data integrity, the discussion on ensuring data integrity and quality across systems in the pharmaceutical engineering report, \"The validation of interfaces is structured into four phases, separated by gates. This allows for better traceability and control of the coordination processes between all parties involved,\" which speaks to the severity and challenges in detecting issues. The likelihood of occurrence is medium to high, influenced by factors such as human error, system glitches, and cyber threats, which are prevalent in healthcare systems. While some integrity issues can be detected through routine checks and validation processes, others, particularly those involving subtle data manipulation by malware, might remain undetected for long periods. Cybersecurity threats have the potential to compromise data integrity, even when they may appear unrelated, as reported in the KMS healthcare report on data integrity in healthcare. They mentioned the malware, for example, can stealthily breach healthcare systems, leading to the corruption of patient files, changes in database records, or the insertion of false information. Such intrusions can remain undetected for extended periods, potentially resulting in misdiagnoses or inaccurate treatment plans, underscoring the severity and likelihood of these issues."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataIntegrityIssues",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SevereClinicalErrors;TrustLoss"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#UnauthorizedModifications;UserErrors"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data integrity issues occur when data is altered or modified in unauthorized ways, leading to incorrect or misleading information. Research indicates that compromised data integrity can lead to severe clinical errors and erosion of trust in the system. Data integrity can be compromised by user errors or malicious activities, which can affect clinical decisions and erode trust in the system. Maintaining integrity involves implementing audit trails, strict access controls, and regular data validation checks to ensure accuracy and completeness.\n\nData integrity is often compromised when a large amount of patient information is transferred across healthcare systems. Minor glitches in data transfer can result in errors and inconsistencies, highlighting the severity and detectability, as integrity issues can directly affect patient care, leading to potential harm or misdiagnosis. They can impact the trustworthiness of clinical data used for decision-making. Considering the numerous risks, ranging from human error during data entry to cyberattacks targeting healthcare systems, Niv Sluzki at IBM discussed the high likelihood and potential severity of data integrity issues arising from cybersecurity threats. He mentioned cyber-attacks are significant threats to data integrity, as cybercriminals can manipulate, delete or steal sensitive information."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataInterceptionAndBreach",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data interception and breaches occur when unauthorized entities access or manipulate data during transmission. Several studies indicate such breaches can compromise patient confidentiality and trust, leading to significant legal and reputational damage. These breaches often arise from vulnerabilities in network security, such as weak encryption or inadequate access controls. Consequences include potential data manipulation and identity theft. Mitigation involves using end-to-end encryption, secure communication protocols such as SSL/TLS, and regular security audits to prevent unauthorized access."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLeakage",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSecurityAndPrivacy"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data leakage occurs through the unintentional exposure of sensitive information due to inadequate data handling or weak security measures, resulting in unauthorized access by unintended parties. Literature identifies several causes, including poor data handling protocols and lack of employee training. The consequences can range from privacy breaches, such as violations of patient confidentiality, to legal implications. Mitigation strategies include enforcing data handling protocols and implementing Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tools to monitor and prevent unauthorized data transfers"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLifecycleManagement",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBased"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLifecycleManagementContext",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedContext"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLifecycleManagementEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLifecycleManagementJustification",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedJustification"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLoss",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#LossOfPatientData;OperationalDowntime;LegalConsequences"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemFailures;InadequateBackups"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data loss occurs when information is permanently deleted, misplaced, or becomes inaccessible due to system failures, inadequate backups, human error, or cyberattacks. Literature studies highlight the severe impact that data loss can have on healthcare operations, including disruptions to patient care continuity and potential legal ramifications. The consequences range from irrecoverable patient records to operational downtime and regulatory penalties. Strategies to prevent data loss include automated backup systems, comprehensive disaster recovery plans, and user training to ensure that data can be reliably restored, thereby minimizing accidental losses.\n\nData loss in healthcare is critically severe because it can lead to irrecoverable patient information, complicating ongoing care and violating compliance requirements. Argaw, Salem T., et al. highlighted this issue through the implications of cyberattacks, such as the May 2017 worldwide WannaCry attack, which serves as a wake-up call; however, it is in the best interest of organizations to maintain vigilance even when threats are not in the headlines. The risk of data loss is ever-present, exacerbated by factors like ransomware attacks or physical damage to storage devices, suggesting a high likelihood. The detectability of data loss can be immediate in cases of catastrophic failures, but may be delayed in more insidious scenarios, such as slow data leaks. To overcome such issues, Almalawi, Abdulmohsen, et al. emphasized the use of encryption and secure data transfer methods. They proposed the LRO-S technique, which encrypts sensitive patient data before storing it in the cloud. The primary goal of their study was to enhance the safety and security of medical professionals’ access to cloud-based patient-sensitive data. However, implementing robust backup solutions and regular data integrity checks can aid in quicker identification."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMinimizationProof",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PersonalDataEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMinimizationProofLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMinimizationProof"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataMisinterpretation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysis"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataOverload",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Excessive data volumes can overwhelm systems, causing performance degradation or data loss. Literature suggests that data overload is a growing concern in high-data environments, such as intensive care units. Overload is typically caused by the transmission of excessive data, resulting in system crashes or slowdowns. The consequences include delayed or lost data, which can impact care quality. Solutions include data prioritization, compression techniques, and scalable cloud-based solutions to manage large data volumes efficiently."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataPipelineVerificationTests",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Transformation-ETLProcessesEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataPipelineVerificationTestsLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataPipelineVerificationTests"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysis",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data processing and analysis are necessary stages in healthcare, where raw data is transformed into actionable insights to inform clinical decisions. However, these stages are prone to various hazards, including technical flaws, biases, and errors in interpretation. Understanding and mitigating these hazards is essential for ensuring the accuracy, reliability, and fairness of data-driven healthcare decisions. The risks associated with data processing and analysis, such as inaccuracies, algorithmic bias, overfitting, and real-time processing failures, can have significant consequences for patient outcomes if not properly managed."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysisCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProfilingLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProfilingReport"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProfilingReport",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/AccuracyAndCompletenessEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQuality",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBased"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQualityClaim",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSubclaim" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/AccuracyAndCompletenessClaim"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TraceabilityClaim"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ValidationClaim"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQuality"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQualityContext",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data pipelines include validation procedures for accuracy and completeness."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedContext"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQualityEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataQualityJustification",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataBasedJustification"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataRestoreAfterCorruption",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataLifecycleManagementContext"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataRestoreAfterCorruptionContext",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataRestoreAfterCorruption"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Determines data can be restored after corruption, loss, or disaster."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataRoutingAndSwitchingErrors",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Routing and switching errors occur when data is incorrectly directed or lost during transmission, resulting in delays or data breaches. Literature points out that these errors can disrupt patient care and compromise data security. Misrouted data is often caused by misconfigurations or network congestion, resulting in privacy breaches and treatment delays. Strategies include the accurate configuration of routing tables, the use of error-checking protocols, and the employment of redundant network paths to ensure data integrity."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSecurityAndPrivacy",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data security and privacy are critical to healthcare systems as they handle sensitive patient information. Protecting this data from unauthorized access, use, or disclosure is crucial for maintaining patient trust and ensuring compliance with legal standards. The hazards, such as unauthorized access, inadequate encryption, compliance failures, data leakage, phishing attacks, and physical security breaches, can lead to severe consequences, including legal penalties, financial losses, and damage to reputation. Implementing comprehensive security measures and conducting regular audits are crucial to mitigating these risks and ensuring the integrity and confidentiality of healthcare data. The security and privacy controls discussed here are aligned with internationally recognised frameworks for health software security. Specifically, it aligns with the FDA Cybersecurity in Medical Devices: Quality System Considerations. This includes integrating security risk management into the software life cycle, implementing secure-by-design architecture and coding practices, ensuring authentication and access control, utilizing cryptography and key management, implementing security logging and monitoring, managing vulnerabilities, patching and updating software, maintaining a software bill of materials (SBOM), responding to incidents, coordinating vulnerability disclosures, and conducting postmarket surveillance. These alignments ensure that the recommended controls support both technical robustness and regulatory expectations."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSecurityAndPrivacyCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStewardshipAssigmentsRecordsEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/GovernanceRolesEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStewardshipAssigmentsRecordsEvidenceLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStewardshipAssigmentsRecordsEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data storage and management are significant for maintaining the integrity, security, and accessibility of healthcare data. Effective management ensures that data is reliable and accessible throughout its lifecycle, supporting continuity of care and compliance with legal and ethical standards.This phase involves several potential hazards like data corruption, loss, duplication, fragmentation, and inadequate backups, which can significantly impact patient safety and operational efficiency."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagementCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagementConsequences",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Consequences"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSychronizationFailures",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data synchronization failures occur when updates are not consistently reflected across systems, leading to discrepancies such as conflicting patient information. Literature indicates these failures can cause outdated or conflicting information, risking patient safety. Synchronization issues often result from inadequate protocols or system conflicts, leading to different data versions across platforms. Implementing robust synchronization protocols and conflict resolution mechanisms can ensure data consistency and accuracy across systems."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransformations",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/GovernanceAndComplianceContext"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransformationsContext",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransformations"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data transformations are tracked, validated, and tested for correctness"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransit",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SecurityAndPrivacy"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransitClaim",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasContext" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EncryptedDataContext"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasEvidence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EncryptionConfigurationsLog"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/KeyManagementPoliciesLog"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TLSCertificatesLog"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/JustifiedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MinimizedExposureRisksJustification"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27002:2022Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/NISTSP800-53Standard"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransit"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Data in transit and at rest is protected"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransitEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SecurityAndPrivacyEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "The transfer and exchange of data between different systems, devices, or organizations are imperative in healthcare, where accurate and secure data transmission ensures patient safety and effective healthcare delivery. However, this process comes with risks such as transmission errors, data breaches, inconsistent formats, latency, which can compromise the reliability, security, and accuracy of healthcare data. Effective mitigation strategies such as robust encryption, standardized data formats, and real-time processing are required to minimize these risks."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharingCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionErrors",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27001:2022Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO27799:2016Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Regulation2017/745Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CyberSecurityInMedicalDevicesStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DICOMStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HealthLevel7FHIRStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IEC81001-5-1Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IHEProfilesStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/NISTAES-256Standard"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataUseInterpretation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "The stage of data use and interpretation is critical in healthcare as it directly influences clinical decisionmaking and patient outcomes. Despite accurate data collection and processing, hazards can arise when data is misinterpreted, overrelied upon, or used out of context. The risks include incorrect clinical decisions, patient harm, and loss of trust in datadriven systems. To ensure data is utilized effectively and safely, healthcare organizations must be aware of these hazards and implement strategies to mitigate them, thereby improving the quality of care and patient safety."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HazardModes"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataUseInterpretationCauses",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PotentialCauses"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataValidationScripts",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ValidationEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
