  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DefenceMechanismsEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ResilienceAgainstAdversarialAttacksEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DelayedDataEntry",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#DelayedTreatments;ImpairedPatientOutcomes"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LowDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemInefficiencies;HighWorkloads"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Timeliness in data entry is crucial for ensuring up-to-date information is available for clinical decisions. Delays, whether due to system inefficiencies or high workloads, can result in outdated data being used for treatment decisions. For example, a delay in entering lab results might lead to incorrect or delayed treatment. Several studies have shown that delayed data entry can compromise patient outcomes, leading to errors that could have been prevented with timely information.\n\nDelayed updates in patient records can lead to harmful gaps in care, especially in time-sensitive situations, as reported in the ECRI Institute report on data errors in health IT systems. The severity is high, particularly in emergency departments where treatment decisions must be made quickly. The likelihood increases (medium to high) in manual record systems or during periods of high patient load. In contrast, detectability remains low unless organizations implement automated time checks for updating patient records, as reported in the Infognana report: ’Time constraints and complexity often lead to delays in data updates, risking patient safety’."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Detectability",
