  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ImplementationDocumentation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RiskMitigitaionStrategiesEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ImplementationDocumentationLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ImplementationDocumentation"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InaccurateDataProcessing",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysis"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inaccurate data processing occurs when errors are introduced during the transformation of raw data into a usable format. These errors can arise from faulty algorithms, incorrect data handling, or software bugs, leading to misclassifications, incorrect estimations, and ultimately, misleading clinical insights. Causes include software bugs, misapplication of algorithms, and human errors in setting up processing pipelines. Consequences range from misleading clinical insights to potential patient harm due to incorrect diagnoses or treatments. Mitigation strategies include robust testing and validation of algorithms, continuous monitoring of data processing workflows, and thorough documentation to prevent errors."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateDataBackupAndRecovery",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ExtendedDowntime;LossOfOperationalDataAndContinuity"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#TechnicalFailures;InsufficientBackupFrequency"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataStorageAndManagement"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inadequate data backup and recovery refer to failures in regularly backing up data or using unreliable methods that don’t ensure data can be restored. Several studies indicate that inadequate backups can lead to prolonged downtime and data loss, significantly impacting healthcare operations. The consequences of technical failures and insufficient backup frequency include irrecoverable data and extended downtimes. Effective strategies involve implementing automated backup systems, storing backups in multiple locations, and regularly testing the integrity of backups.\n\nThe failure to effectively back up and perform data recovery can result in significant data loss with critical implications for patient care and compliance with health regulations. Its impact can be very high, as discussed in Open Access Government news about the COVID-19 pandemic, which resulted in an immediate demand for large-scale data modeling and visualization to track spikes in cases. This dramatic increase in data being written and read requires a scalable file storage system to store large data sets, underscoring the critical need for robust and scalable backup and recovery systems to handle sudden increases in data usage, which impacts the severity of inadequate practices. The likelihood and detectability can be medium, depending on the organization’s adherence to best practices in data management and the robustness of their IT infrastructure."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateDataNormalization",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysis"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inadequate data normalization leads to incorrect analysis due to the failure to standardize data before processing. This issue can result in skewed insights and misinterpretation of clinical data. Literature highlights causes including inconsistent data formats, improper handling of outliers, and lack of preprocessing, with consequences of distorted analysis and misleading clinical decisions. Mitigation involves implementing consistent data normalization protocols and careful preprocessing steps to ensure data accuracy and comparability."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InadequateEncryption",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC27799Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Regulation2017/745Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/CyberSecurityInMedicalDevicesStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DICOMStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HealthLevel7FHIRStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IEC81001-5-1Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IHEProfilesStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/NISTAES-256Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TLS1.3Regulation"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataSecurityAndPrivacy"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inadequate encryption refers to outdated or flawed encryption methods that fail to protect data during storage or transmission, making it vulnerable to unauthorized access. Literature highlights causes such as outdated encryption algorithms and poor key management. This can result in exposure of sensitive data and increased risk of data breaches. Mitigation strategies include adopting strong encryption algorithms such as AES-256 or post-quantum standards, ensuring secure key management, and regularly updating encryption practices to counter emerging threats. Cryptographic protections are based on NIST AES-256 and TLS 1.3, and can be implemented within an ISO/IEC 27001-governed information security management system. Sector-specific requirements can be addressed via IEC 81001-5-1 (health software security) and ISO/IEC27799 (information security management in health), while interoperability layers reference HL7 FHIR, IHE profiles, and DICOM to maintain secure, auditable exchange. In addition, practices can be cross-checked against the FDA Cybersecurity in Medical Devices guidance and MDR 2017/745 to ensure technical controls underpin regulatory compliance."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentOrBiasDiscrepancyReports",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DocumentedAndAddressDiscrepanciesEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentOrBiasDiscrepancyReportsClaim",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentOrBiasDiscrepancyReports"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseDocumentation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/ThresholdViolationsForSafetyMechnanismsEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseDocumentationLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseDocumentation"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseLogs"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseLogs",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/GeneratedAlertsEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseProcedures",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PoisonedDataDetectionMethodsEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseProceduresLog",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncidentResponseProcedures"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncompleteDataCollection",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SuboptimalCare;Mis-judgment"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LowDetectability"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SystemLimitations;HumanOversight"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Missing or partial data can result from system limitations, human oversight, or faulty sensors, leaving critical gaps in patient information. This insufficiency hinders effective clinical decision-making, potentially leading to suboptimal care. Literature on healthcare data emphasizes the importance of comprehensive data collection, noting that incomplete data can skew clinical judgments and adversely affect patient outcomes.\n\nIncomplete or missing patient data, such as medical history or allergy information, leads to significant treatment risks or even life-threatening situations. The ECRI Institute reported that these potential risks to patients are associated with selection escape detection, in which the patient might receive an intravenous solution, intended as a one-time dose, every hour for six hours, instead of a 20 mL/kg bolus of 5% dextrose solution over eight hours. Due to incomplete data in the computerized provider order entry (CPOE) system that was not detected during pharmacist order verification or nurse order review, the patient received 20 mL/kg each hour for six hours [10]. Hence, severity may be high in these types of situations, particularly for treatments that require complete medical histories, allergies, or other key data. The likelihood or occurrence is medium to high, depending on how well the system prompts for required fields during data entry [156]. Felix et al. discussed a framework to detect occurrence, initially proposed by Shang et al., for several measures to recognize the issues related to data quality (e.g., completeness, accuracy, and timeliness). Detectability is usually low unless routine audits or automatic completeness checks are in place. The 4medica report highlighted that poor data quality has low detectability with profound implications for patient well-being and satisfaction. When data is incomplete or inconsistent, it can lead to incorrect diagnoses, inappropriate treatments, and unnecessary medical tests. Such mistakes can cause frustration and confusion among patients, who rightfully expect accurate and personalized care."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InconsistentDataFormats",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DICOMStandard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HealthLevel7FHIRStandard"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataTransmissionAndSharing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inconsistent data formats occur when different systems use varying formats, leading to integration issues. Literature emphasizes that this can lead to misinterpretation or data loss, thereby affecting the quality of care. These inconsistencies often stem from the lack of standardized data formats across systems, resulting in errors during data interpretation or transmission. Adopting standards like HL7 or DICOM and using middleware for data format conversion are effective mitigation measures."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InconsistentDataLabeling",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataProcessingAndAnalysis"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inconsistent labeling during data processing can confuse model training and lead to inaccurate outputs. These errors occur while tagging data with meaningful labels, either manually or automatically. Literature highlights the leading causes as manual labeling and flaws in automated labeling tools. Consequences include model misclassification and reduced accuracy, while mitigation strategies involve developing standardized data labeling processes and implementing quality checks to ensure label consistency and improve model training and performance."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InconsistentInterpretationsAndDataDrift",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataUseInterpretation"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Inconsistent interpretations arise from ambiguous guidelines and varying adoption rates of new practices, resulting in diverse clinical decisions and variations in care quality. In contrast, data drift refers to changes in interpretation over time, which can lead to inconsistent decision-making. Several studies highlighted that ambiguous guidelines, lack of standardization, and evolving clinical knowledge contribute to inconsistent care and potential errors. Standardizing guidelines, providing regular updates and training, and implementing peer review systems are effective in minimizing these inconsistencies and ensuring uniformity in data interpretation."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IncorrectDataEntry",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#Misdiagnoses;IncorrectTreatmentPlans"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumToHighLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#SuboptimalCare;Mis-judgment"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Errors during data entry, whether manual or automated, are a significant hazard. Manual data entry is particularly prone to errors such as typographical mistakes, incorrect coding, and selection errors in dropdown menus. Automated data capture, though designed to minimize errors, can still introduce inaccuracies due to sensor malfunctions, incorrect calibration, or data synchronization failures. These errors can lead to misdiagnoses, incorrect treatment plans, and delays in patient care, as supported by studies highlighting the cascading effects of initial data inaccuracies on clinical outcomes.\n\n\nIncorrect data entry is a significant concern in healthcare, particularly in systems where manual data entry is prevalent. The severity of this issue is high, as errors in data entry can result in incorrect diagnoses. An ECRI Institute report on data errors in health IT systems reveals that human-computer interface issues are the most frequent cause of data entry errors, accounting for 65% of health IT-related incidents. Minor errors can escalate into severe patient safety risks if undetected. The likelihood of these errors being medium to high, as mentioned in the Institute for Healthcare Improvement (IHI) report. ’If the detection of failure takes too long, it may become too late to repair the situation, and the magnitude of the problem will become much greater’. However, the likelihood of these errors is heightened in busy environments, with detectability varying but is often medium depending on the presence of cross-checks or validation tools in place."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Individual",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Stakeholder"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InformationOverload",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataUseInterpretation"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Information overload occurs when healthcare professionals deal with more data than they can effectively manage, leading to potential oversights and decision-making failures. Literature highlights that excessive data, inadequate filtering tools, and a lack of integration contribute to decision immobility and the omission of critical information. Key strategies for managing these issues include implementing data summarization tools, customizable dashboards for prioritizing information, and training on effective data management to prevent overload and enhance decision-making efficiency."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InputsPredicitonsAndConfidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DecisionLogging"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InputsPredicitonsAndConfidenceClaim",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasContext" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/IntegratedLoggingToolsContext"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasEvidence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DecisionLog"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/PeriodicReviewDocumentationLog"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/StakeholderFeedbackReportsLog"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Art13Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Art14Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/Art15Standard"
  }, {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/NISTSP800-53AU-2"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InputsPredicitonsAndConfidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InputsPredicitonsAndConfidenceEvidence",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DecisionLoggingEvidence"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/InputsPredictionsAndConfidenceClaim",
