  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SupplyChainAndThird-PartyRisks",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazardsModes"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "Healthcare organizations often rely on third-party vendors for critical services and supplies, such as software or hardware embedding, making them vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and vendor unreliability, which can impact healthcare data systems. Literature highlights vendor failures as the primary cause of these risks. The consequences include prolonged system outages and compromised data security. Mitigation strategies involve diversifying suppliers, ensuring third-party compliance with security standards, and incorporating contingency plans for vendor-related disruptions."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SystemDesignAndUserInterfaceIssuesHazard",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasConsequence" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#IncreasedEntryErrors;UserDissatisfaction"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasDetectability" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/LowDetectability"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasLikelihood" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/MediumLikelihood"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasPotentialCause" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#PoorlyDesignedInterfaces;LackOfFeedback"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HasSeverity" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/HighSeverity"
  } ],
  "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD#ISO/IEC62366-1:2015Standard"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/DataEntryAndCollection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "The design of data entry systems and user interfaces plays a crucial role in preventing or causing hazards. Complex or non-intuitive interfaces can contribute to data entry errors. If a system lacks confirmation mechanisms or fails to provide adequate feedback, users may unknowingly submit incorrect data. Research highlights the importance of user-centered design in healthcare systems to minimize errors and enhance data accuracy, with poorly designed interfaces associated with increased user error and patient risk. IEC 62366-1:2015 requires a systematic approach to usability engineering for medical devices, such as Usability Engineering Process Integration for user specification and user interface evaluation, Use-Related Risk Analysis for critical tasks identification, user error analysis, and risk Measures for Usability, and Human Factors Integration for cognitive load assessment, environmental factors, and user characteristics.\n\nThe system design significantly impacts everything in healthcare settings, from usability to patient safety, and can lead to substantial risks if flawed. Masci et al. discussed the formal verification of medical device user interfaces, highlighting the potential consequences of interface issues on patient outcomes and the commonality of these errors due to complex human-machine interactions. The severity of system and interface design flaws can be high due to their potential to cause incorrect medical decisions or delays in treatment. In contrast, the likelihood of these errors is relatively common given the complexity and varied use environments of healthcare systems. The detectability of these issues can be challenging as they often require rigorous testing and user feedback, which are not always adequately performed in the developmental stages of medical device software."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/SystemOutages",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/EnvironmentalFactorsInDataHazardsModes"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "System outages in healthcare can result from power failures, network disruptions, server crashes, or natural disasters, leading to significant disruptions in clinical services and data accessibility. Literature highlights several causes, such as insufficient power backups, overloaded servers, and a lack of disaster recovery plans. The consequences are severe, including data corruption, service disruptions, and delayed patient care. Mitigation strategies involve implementing uninterruptible power supplies (UPS), conducting regular server maintenance, and establishing comprehensive disaster recovery protocols to minimize downtime and data loss."
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TLS",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/RegulatoryBody"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/ek279783/ontologies/2025/8/ontologySD/TLS1.3",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
